course: dictionary
lesson: 2163
title: Cornish: a place and a language
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some words can work as different parts of speech. «Bronze is copper mixed with tin, and Cornish tin was traded across Europe for thousands of years.» Here Cornish is an adjective. It describes the tin and tells us where it came from: Cornwall.
explain: Cornish can also be a noun, the name of a language. The Cornish language is the old Celtic language of Cornwall, and people are learning it again today. Names of languages start with a capital letter, just like English and Welsh.
explain: To tell which meaning fits, look at the words around it. Ask yourself: is Cornish describing a thing, like a pasty or a beach, or is it the name of something? If you put «Cornish» into the sentence and it is describing a noun, it is an adjective.
fact: Cornish (adjective) means from or to do with Cornwall, a county in the far south-west of England.
fact: Cornish (noun) means the old Celtic language of Cornwall, which is being learned again.
fact: Cornish is a Celtic language related to Welsh and Breton.
fact: Names of languages, such as English, Welsh and Cornish, start with a capital letter.
fact: Cornish tin mines were important in history, and Cornish tin was traded across Europe.
exercise: «Bronze is copper mixed with tin, and Cornish tin was traded across Europe for thousands of years.» In this sentence, what kind of word is Cornish? | Plural noun, a group of people | Verb, meaning to trade | *Adjective, describing the tin | Noun, the name of a language
exercise: Ella says, «Cornish pasties are famous.» What does Cornish describe here? | A kind of cream tea | *The pasties, showing they come from Cornwall | A county in the north of England | The language the pasties are written in
exercise: Which language is Cornish related to? | French and German | Spanish and Italian | *Welsh and Breton | English and Scots
test: «Cornish beaches are full of families in the summer.» What does Cornish describe? | The families' language | A type of boat | Something to eat | *The beaches, showing they are in Cornwall
test: Why does Cornish start with a capital letter when it means the language? | It is a very important word | It is the name of a person | It is the first word of every sentence | *It is the name of a language, and names of languages start with a capital
exam: Which is the best meaning of Cornish as a noun? | A kind of cream tea | A type of tin mine that is no longer used | *The old Celtic language of Cornwall, which is being learned again | A county in the far north of Scotland
